GetFieldRes addHashFieldToReply(client *c, kvobj *o, sds field, int hfeFlags) {
    if (o == NULL) {
        addReplyNull(c);
        return GETF_NOT_FOUND;
    }

    unsigned char *vstr = NULL;
    unsigned int vlen = UINT_MAX;
    long long vll = LLONG_MAX;

    GetFieldRes res = hashTypeGetValue(c->db, o, field, &vstr, &vlen, &vll, hfeFlags, NULL);
    if (res == GETF_OK) {
        if (vstr) {
            addReplyBulkCBuffer(c, vstr, vlen);
        } else {
            addReplyBulkLongLong(c, vll);
        }
    } else {
        addReplyNull(c);
    }
    return res;
}

char buf[MAX_LONG_DOUBLE_CHARS];
int len = ld2string(buf,sizeof(buf),value,LD_STR_HUMAN);
new = sdsnewlen(buf,len);
hashTypeSet(c->db, o,c->argv[2]->ptr,new,HASH_SET_TAKE_VALUE);
addReplyBulkCBuffer(c,buf,len);

robj *newobj;
newobj = createRawStringObject(buf,len);
rewriteClientCommandVector(c, 6, shared.hsetex, c->argv[1], shared.keepttl,
                    shared.fields, shared.integers[1], c->argv[2], newobj);
decrRefCount(newobj);

findings.body:
The rewrite passes seven objects (`shared.hsetex`, `c->argv[1]`, `shared.keepttl`, `shared.fields`, `shared.integers[1]`, `c->argv[2]`, `newobj`) but sets `argc` to 6. `rewriteClientCommandVector` in `src/networking.c` reads exactly `argc` varargs, so the last object (the new value) is never copied. The propagated command becomes `HSETEX key KEEPTTL FIELDS 1 field` with no value. Replicas and AOF replay will reject it or apply an invalid write, which breaks replication for every HINCRBYFLOAT.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2571-2572"}

::suggestion{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2571-2572"}
rewriteClientCommandVector(c, 7, shared.hsetex, c->argv[1], shared.keepttl,
                    shared.fields, shared.integers[1], c->argv[2], newobj);

The existing `rewriteClientCommandVector` calls in `src/t_string.c` use the same pattern, with argc matching the object count.

The local write used to be `hashTypeSet(..., HASH_SET_TAKE_VALUE | HASH_SET_KEEP_TTL)`. The PR drops the KEEP_TTL flag. In `hashTypeSet` (`src/t_hash.c`), without that flag the hashtable path calls `hfieldPersist` on the existing field, and the listpack path calls `listpackExUpdateExpiry(..., HASH_LP_NO_TTL)`. Either way the master removes the field's expiration.

Failure scenario: `HSETEX h EX 100 FIELDS 1 f 1` followed by `HINCRBYFLOAT h f 1.1`. Before the PR, `HTTL h FIELDS 1 f` returns about 100 on the master. After the PR it returns -1. The replica then also ends up with no TTL, since the stream carries KEEPTTL and nothing to keep. The PR fixes replica divergence by introducing master-side data loss.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2559-2559"}

::suggestion{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2559-2559"}
hashTypeSet(c->db, o,c->argv[2]->ptr,new,HASH_SET_TAKE_VALUE | HASH_SET_KEEP_TTL);

The new test asserts the replication stream, which expects `{hsetex h1 KEEPTTL FIELDS 1 f1 *}`. With the argc bug, the actual line has six tokens, so the assertion fails. The test also never checks the master's TTL after `hincrbyfloat h1 f1 1.1`. That is why the KEEP_TTL removal went unnoticed. The only TTL assertions after the increment are on the replica.

::code{file="tests/unit/type/hash-field-expire.tcl" lines="1970-1976"}

Add an assertion that the master's `HTTL` for `f1` is still within 90..100 after the increment, and make the test fail if the stream line is malformed.
